reasoning:
PRO wins a competitive debate on its central reliability claim. Physical controls give tactile confirmation and still work when the touch layer, rendering, or capacitive input degrades. That claim stayed live through the whole exchange.

CON ran the more aggressive rebuttal game. It pressed hard on PRO narrowing its scope from all visibility, traction, and signaling functions to a few core controls. It also capitalized on PRO's line that "a screen and a switch are both visual." Those were real hits.

CON's affirmative mechanism, however, rested on overconfident and largely unsupported claims:
- that gloves and damp fingers "register reliably";
- that a bezel-braced tap is "deterministic";
- that screens are necessarily higher-mounted than switches;
- that physical controls signal only through "tiny LEDs."

PRO correctly flagged the "tiny LEDs" description as a misreading of its proposal. CON's "same body-control computer" point also sidesteps PRO's actual claim, which was about display and touch failure, not wiring.

PRO's prioritization answer is a defensible reading of the motion's "core functions": keep the urgent actions physical and let secondary modes live elsewhere. CON's claim that fan control was pushed onscreen partly overstated what PRO actually said.

Both sides cited nonexistent P-prefixed reference IDs, a minor and symmetric cost. Overall, PRO's case is better grounded and closer to the motion. CON's case leaned on best-case screen performance in exactly the degraded conditions at issue.

Both sides were disciplined and well grounded, and the round stayed close. CON edged it because it repeatedly turned PRO's own framing against it. It used PRO's cereal arithmetic to show that unit prices already capture the change. It built the A/B shelf comparison: the cheaper shrunken box gets flagged while the dearer price-hiked box gets nothing. It also used PRO's temporary-notice design to argue the benefit is rationed by timing while compliance costs persist.

PRO's best line held up reasonably well: habitual buyers anchor on the headline price, so a steady headline masks a change in value. Its A1/A2 answers were direct and honest. However, PRO never fully neutralized three things:
- the bias concern that the flag targets how a rise is delivered;
- the "diagnosis without treatment" point, since only the current unit price tells shoppers which way to switch;
- the administrative burden of deciding product identity, which PRO kept deferring to unspecified "careful rules."

PRO could also have exploited CON's alternative ("make unit prices bigger and uniform"), which arguably concedes part of the motion, but it largely let this pass. CON's weighing was crisper and more memorable, giving the reader a cleaner reason to vote.

PRO was the more inventive and quotable side. It made three strong moves: framing the debate around scarce enforcement, turning CON's deterrence point (penalty times probability), and arguing that siting hotspots by spike volume would fall disproportionately on this class.

That progression, however, steadily shed the motion's actual comparator. In stages, PRO:
- dropped luxury as a category ("capability, not chrome");
- exempted quiet luxury EVs from noise scrutiny;
- folded modified ordinary commuters into the target group;
- conceded that cameras should be sited strictly by spike volume;
- said officer screening is "conduct underway, not bodywork."

CON consistently and accurately named this as circularity. If loud ordinary cars count as "performance" and quiet luxury cars do not, then conduct, not vehicle class, concentrates the harm. On that reading, the motion's call for tighter enforcement of luxury and performance cars than of ordinary vehicles goes unsupported.

PRO's best surviving route was the sub-limit launch: an officer needs a "prior," and capability supplies it. CON blunted this route in two ways:
- **Visibility:** PRO's stated priors (a cabin sport button, the hidden power of a silent EV) are invisible from the curb.
- **Uniform alternative:** an officer watching the stop line catches any car that lunges, whatever its badge.

PRO's closing claim that hotspot enforcement *is* tighter enforcement of the class relies on disparate impact rather than class-based tightening. It also rests on an unsupported assertion that complaints cluster around valved exhausts, whereas CON asserted that aftermarket mods and motorcycles dominate.

Neither side's empirics were verifiable. Overall, CON left the cleaner decision rule, with PRO stronger on rhetoric but weaker in fidelity to the motion.

PRO had the more vivid framing and an early win in pointing out the tension between "revenue is drawn overwhelmingly from adults" and "caps crush studios." CON, however, built the more durable route to victory: a verification dilemma. Truly youth-targeted defaults require either parental opt-in, which already exists, or identity gates on every player, which cost adult funders. CON also pressed a concrete refund-exploit problem, and PRO effectively let that point go.

PRO's mechanism kept moving under that pressure:
- verified age and bans on minors buying randomized pulls;
- then payment-channel attestation;
- then a universal ceiling liftable with one click;
- then, in the closing, a ceiling liftable only with the adult's own credential.

CON exploited the one-click version well. In closing, CON also correctly noted that default ceilings on adult cards break PRO's promise that "every adult keeps every flexible option." PRO's "your best evidence limits work is a limit" line is clever, but it doesn't rescue a plan that can no longer target youth without the costs CON identified. On balance, CON made the decisive clash clearer and held its position more consistently, though PRO's rhetoric keeps this close.

This was a close debate, and CON wins narrowly on direct engagement.

PRO had the more memorable framing. "Stored time," the irreversibility asymmetry, and "the emergency is the alibi" all land well. PRO's pressure questions also earned two real concessions: CON admitted a ban is easier to police and that public bottling is possible.

CON's questions were equally productive. PRO conceded that monitoring costs are the same under either regime and that public operation saves no water in a given season. That shrank PRO's live case to one claim: a for-profit extractor is an adversary that will capture the safe-yield reading over time.

CON answered that remaining claim with specific mechanisms:
- the authority selects the monitor;
- the valve stays closed by default unless fresh readings support the draw;
- statutory suspension triggers cannot be waived locally;
- grants are nontransferable, with no renewal right;
- no pumping is allowed during an appeal.

PRO's later speeches mostly restated the adversary concern rather than showing why those mechanisms fail. PRO also leaned on mischaracterizations that CON corrected cleanly: "deed," "continuously, forever," and relabeling CON's model as "public bottling." CON also held its distinction between ownership, trading, and extraction, which kept the overbreadth of the motion's extraction clause live.

PRO's weighing — avoided machinery costs versus an irreplaceable aquifer — is strong rhetorically. However, it assumes the controls fail, and CON contested that assumption more concretely.

PRO (B) wins because it built a concrete, layered case and kept adapting it. Its opening offered a costly-signal test: VAT, subsidy cuts and legal reforms create domestic losers and buy no applause. It backed that with real operating exporters such as aluminium, Ma'aden fertiliser, DP World and ACWA Power. In rebuttal it turned CON's own "independent revenue" test against CON by pointing to foreign buyers who owe Riyadh nothing. It also exposed CON's criterion as absorbing every fact.

CON (A) was disciplined and honest. It answered the threshold question cleanly and admitted it was not claiming below-cost sales. Its "direction versus motion" and "net of public support" distinctions were genuinely sharp. But CON offered almost no affirmative evidence that diversification is failing or is mostly branding. Its case was entirely burden-shifting.

CON also conceded a great deal along the way:
- real ports and factories;
- a genuine commercial hub;
- that some efforts are already substantive;
- that unfinished does not mean fake.

Those concessions weaken a CON position on a motion framed as "substantive and likely to succeed rather than mostly PR." PRO's trajectory evidence (VAT tripling, corporate tax, the tourism target reached early, rising industrial input prices) was plausible and directly responsive. CON's reply that VAT just taxes oil-funded consumption was clever but only partial. The debate was competitive, but PRO made it easier to decide.

B wins decisively. B put the motion's concession clause at the center of the debate: are response-time increases an acceptable price? B then pressed that question consistently through every round.

A's dual-dispatch defense backfired. B showed it gives up A's opening promise of reallocating police resources, and it makes the delay irrelevant to the claimed benefits. That produced a clean dilemma: either clinicians go alone, so the delay is the whole response, or police are present, so no patrol time is saved.

A leaned heavily on brittle fake precision:
- an unnamed pilot with a 60% shooting reduction and $1.2M in savings;
- 57% fewer arrests and 68% fewer injuries;
- a named Austin example with an "18-minute knife call";
- a 15% drop in overdose deaths.

These came with escalating specificity and no grounding. The Austin claim was also introduced late, which widened the debate.

A's Pressure Questions were misdirected. Its Q2 asked B to defend a claim A itself had made. A also never answered B's selection-bias point about screened calls or the point that police and fire crews already carry naloxone.

A's closing repeated unsupported figures and inflated "twenty minutes slower" into a supposed proof of benefit. It did not engage B's dilemma. B's speeches were tight and adaptive, and B's closing turned A's own words against A.
